#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:323
msgid "Using Supervisord"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:324
msgid ""
"Go to ``/etc/supervisor/conf.d/`` and create a new file named ``syncthing."
"conf`` with the following content (ensure you replaced ``<USERNAME>`` with "
"valid username)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:326
msgid ""
"[program:syncthing]\n"
"autorestart = True\n"
"directory = /home/<USERNAME>/\n"
"user = <USERNAME>\n"
"command = /usr/bin/syncthing --no-browser\n"
"environment = STNORESTART=\"1\", HOME=\"/home/<USERNAME>\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:333
msgid "Reload Supervisord::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:335
msgid "supervisorctl reload"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:337
msgid "Then start it::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:339
msgid "supervisorctl start syncthing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:341
msgid "and check it is all working::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:343
msgid "supervisorctl status syncthing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:345
msgid "In case of troubles check the logs::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:347
msgid "supervisorctl tail syncthing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:350
msgid "Using systemd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:352
msgid ""
"Systemd is a suite of system management daemons, libraries, and utilities "
"designed as a central management and configuration platform for the Linux "
"computer operating system. It also offers users the ability to manage "
"services under the user's control with a per-user systemd instance, enabling "
"users to start, stop, enable, and disable their own units. Service files for "
"systemd are provided by Syncthing, either in the ``etc/linux-systemd`` "
"directory of the downloaded tarball or directly installed to the appropriate "
"location on your system when installed through `apt.syncthing.net <https://"
"apt.syncthing.net>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:362
msgid ""
"You have two primary options: You can set up Syncthing as a system service, "
"or a user service."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:365
msgid ""
"Running Syncthing as a system service ensures that Syncthing is run at "
"startup even if the Syncthing user has no active session. Since the system "
"service keeps Syncthing running even without an active user session, it is "
"intended to be used on a *server*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:370
msgid ""
"Running Syncthing as a user service ensures that Syncthing only starts after "
"the user has logged into the system (e.g., via the graphical login screen, "
"or ssh). Thus, the user service is intended to be used on a *(multiuser) "
"desktop computer*. It avoids unnecessarily running Syncthing instances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:375
msgid ""
"The official `Debian/Ubuntu Syncthing repository <https://apt.syncthing.net/"
">`__, and several distros (including Arch Linux) ship these service files "
"along with the Syncthing package. If your distro provides the systemd "
"service files for Syncthing, you can skip step #2 when you're setting up "
"either the system service or the user service, as described below.  If in "
"doubt, try if it works without and go back to step #2 only if enabling the "
"service failed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:383
msgid "How to set up a system service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:385
msgid "Create the user who should run the service, or choose an existing one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:386
msgid ""
"(Skip if your distribution package already installs these files, see above.) "
"From `git location <https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing/raw/main/etc/"
"linux-systemd/system/>`__ copy the ``syncthing@.service`` file into the "
"`load path of the system instance <https://www.freedesktop.org/software/"
"systemd/man/systemd.unit.html#Unit%20File%20Load%20Path>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:390
msgid ""
"Enable and start the service. Replace \"myuser\" with the actual Syncthing "
"user after the ``@``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:393
msgid ""
"systemctl enable syncthing@myuser.service\n"
"systemctl start syncthing@myuser.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:397
msgid "How to set up a user service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:399
msgid ""
"Create the user who should run the service, or choose an existing one. "
"*Probably this will be your own user account.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:401
msgid ""
"(Skip if your distribution package already installs these files, see above.) "
"From `git location <https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing/raw/main/etc/"
"linux-systemd/user/>`__ copy the ``syncthing.service`` file into the `load "
"path of the user instance <https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/"
"systemd.unit.html#Unit%20File%20Load%20Path>`__. To do this without root "
"privileges you can just use this folder under your home directory: ``~/."
"config/systemd/user/``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:407
msgid "Enable and start the service::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:409
msgid ""
"systemctl --user enable syncthing.service\n"
"systemctl --user start syncthing.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:411
msgid ""
"If your home directory is encrypted with eCryptfs on Debian/Ubuntu, then you "
"will need to make the change described in `Ubuntu bug 1734290 <https://bugs."
"launchpad.net/ecryptfs/+bug/1734290>`__. Otherwise the user service will not "
"start, because by default, systemd checks for user services before your home "
"directory has been decrypted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:416
msgid ""
"Automatic start-up of syncthing user services at boot (instead of at login) "
"is possible through systemd's \"lingering\" function, if a system service is "
"not used.  This activates all enabled services for enable-linger users at "
"boot time::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:421
msgid ""
"sudo loginctl enable-linger myuser\n"
"systemctl --user enable syncthing.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:424
msgid ""
"For more information, refer to the `enable-linger`_ command of ``loginctl`` "
"to allow this for a particular user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:430
msgid "Checking the service status"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:432
msgid ""
"To check if Syncthing runs properly you can use the ``status`` subcommand. "
"To check the status of a system service::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:435
msgid "systemctl status syncthing@myuser.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:437
msgid "To check the status of a user service::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:439
msgid "systemctl --user status syncthing.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:442
msgid "Using the journal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:444
msgid ""
"Systemd logs everything into the journal, so you can easily access Syncthing "
"log messages. In both of the following examples, ``-e`` tells the pager to "
"jump to the very end, so that you see the most recent logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:448
msgid "To see the logs for the system service::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:450
msgid "journalctl -e -u syncthing@myuser.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:452
msgid "To see the logs for the user service::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:454
msgid "journalctl -e --user-unit=syncthing.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:459
msgid "Permissions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:461
msgid ""
"If you enabled the ``Ignore Permissions`` option in the Syncthing client's "
"folder settings, then you will also need to add the line ``UMask=0002`` (or "
"any other `umask setting <https://www.tech-faq.com/umask.html>`_ you like) "
"in the ``[Service]`` section of the ``syncthing@.service`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:466
msgid ""
"For the :doc:`/advanced/folder-sync-ownership` option to work, you can grant "
"extra capabilities to the service via the systemd unit file. Add the "
"following snippet to the service file (commented out in the provided "
"template).  To ensure smooth upgrades, keeping it in an override file using "
"``systemctl edit ...`` is advised::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:472
msgid ""
"[Service]\n"
"AmbientCapabilities=CAP_CHOWN CAP_FOWNER"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:476
msgid "Remote-mounted home directories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:478
msgid ""
"If you set up a system service for one or more users whose home directory is "
"mounted over the network (e.g. via NFS), then syncthing will fail to start "
"on boot, unless you tell systemd to delay launching syncthing services until "
"after the remote filesystems are mounted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:484
msgid ""
"To correct this, adjust the syncthing service by creating an override file "
"using ``systemctl edit syncthing@.service``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:487
msgid ""
"# Wait for NFS-mounted home directories to become available\n"
"# before starting syncthing on boot.\n"
"[Unit]\n"
"After=remote-fs.target"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:492
msgid ""
"Note that this is not necessary for lingering user services, which already "
"wait for remote filesystems (if any) to be available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:496
msgid "Debugging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:498
msgid ""
"If you are asked on the bugtracker to start Syncthing with specific "
"environment variables it will not work the normal way. Systemd isolates each "
"service and it cannot access global environment variables. The solution is "
"to add the variables to the service file instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:503
msgid "To edit the system service, run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:505
msgid "systemctl edit syncthing@myuser.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:507
msgid "To edit the user service, run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:509
msgid "systemctl --user edit syncthing.service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/autostart.rst:511
msgid ""
"This will create an additional configuration file automatically and you can "
"define (or overwrite) further service parameters like e.g. "
"``Environment=STTRACE=model``."
msgstr ""
